orcshot (0.1.0-16) noble; urgency=medium

  * Fix the remaining residual monitor jump/wrong-size left over after
    0.1.0-15 - live-confirmed (direflail, 2026-08-22) that deferring
    the destination picker's own dispatch by one main-loop iteration
    reduced but didn't fully eliminate it ("grows somewhat bigger" now
    instead of gigantic). Root cause: a real, mouse-driven menu
    popdown's own X11 grab-release/focus-revert sequence can still
    take Cinnamon/Muffin's placement-mode=automatic window manager
    longer than one main-loop iteration to actually settle on a final
    monitor, so the editor's one-shot monitor check could still read a
    not-yet-final monitor and size itself for the wrong one. Rather
    than guess a fixed delay long enough to outlast that (fragile
    against exactly this class of timing-dependent WM behavior, and
    liable to reintroduce the same bug on a different machine), the
    editor now reacts to its own configure-event and re-adapts its
    size/position every time its actual monitor genuinely changes,
    however many times or however late the window manager moves it.
    Verified live: simulated a delayed cross-monitor move after the
    window had already settled, confirmed it correctly resized and
    re-centered for the new monitor rather than staying stuck with
    stale sizing from the old one.

 -- Orcshot <314918217+artificialorctelligence@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 22 Aug 2026 23:00:00 -0500

orcshot (0.1.0-15) noble; urgency=medium

  * Fix the real cause of the editor's monitor jump - 0.1.0-13/-14's
    own fixes addressed the editor's own initial-placement code but
    direflail's live re-testing still showed the identical double-jump
    afterward ("same behavior"), which turned out to have a different
    root cause entirely: the classic destination picker's own menu
    item handler called its destination's action (Edit, Save As,
    Print, any configured External Command - all of them, not just
    Edit) directly and synchronously from inside the menu's "activate"
    signal, before the menu's own popdown had actually reached the
    display server - the exact same class of race app.py's own
    _defer() was already built and shipped to fix for tray captures
    (see that function's own docstring), just never applied to this
    picker. Starting a brand new top-level window while the menu's
    own X11 grab/focus state hadn't been released yet confused
    Cinnamon/Muffin's placement-mode=automatic + focus-new-windows=
    smart window-manager policy into a visible double-placement.
    Confirmed via a live repro through the real picker menu itself
    (not just the editor window in isolation, which never showed the
    race) that deferring the handler by one main-loop iteration - the
    same fix _defer() already uses - eliminates it: stable from the
    first frame through the real code path, 23 consecutive checks
    over 1.15 seconds with no movement.

 -- Orcshot <314918217+artificialorctelligence@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 22 Aug 2026 22:45:00 -0500

orcshot (0.1.0-14) noble; urgency=medium

  * Fix a follow-up double-jump from 0.1.0-13's own fix (direflail,
    live-testing, 2026-08-22): pinning the editor's initial placement
    to the primary monitor fought Cinnamon/Muffin's own
    placement-mode=automatic window-manager policy - the window opened
    on primary for a moment, then the WM's own placement decision
    moved it again to wherever the mouse actually was, a visible
    double-jump. Checked what real Windows Greenshot does here
    (ImageEditorForm.cs's GetAvailableScreenSpace): it never requests
    a position at all - it uses Screen.FromControl(this), whatever
    monitor Windows' own default placement already chose, and just
    sizes to fit that. The editor now does the same: no explicit
    initial position on either axis, letting the window manager make
    one single placement decision, with _resize_canvas_and_window's
    own existing monitor-detection adapting to wherever that lands -
    confirmed live, stable from the first frame, no more double-jump.

