orcshot (0.1.0-13) noble; urgency=medium

  * Fix the editor window opening on the wrong monitor and growing to
    fill it - live-confirmed (direflail, 2026-08-22, dual monitor:
    2560x1440 primary + 1920x1080 secondary): "opening edit caused the
    window to open then jump to the other monitor and grow almost to
    the size of the screen." Root cause, confirmed by reading the
    actual pointer position at the moment of the bug: the editor's
    initial placement used Gtk.WindowPosition.CENTER, which centers a
    parent-less window on whichever monitor currently has the *mouse
    pointer* - not the primary monitor, not wherever the capture/Edit
    click happened. A hotkey capture, or the mouse simply drifting
    before the editor finished opening, put it on an unrelated
    monitor. The editor now explicitly places itself on the primary
    monitor instead, matching what its own post-capture resize step
    already falls back to - deterministic regardless of where the
    pointer happens to be. Confirmed live: the window now opens on the
    primary monitor and stays there even with the pointer sitting on
    the other one.

 -- Orcshot <314918217+artificialorctelligence@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 22 Aug 2026 22:15:00 -0500

orcshot (0.1.0-12) noble; urgency=medium

  * Fix Quit doing nothing at all when a dialog (Preferences, or an
    Add/Edit External Command dialog) was open - live-confirmed
    (direflail, 2026-08-22: captured, opened Edit, opened Preferences,
    Quit did nothing). Root cause, confirmed via an isolated repro:
    Gtk.Dialog.run() runs its own nested main loop, and
    Gio.Application.quit() only returns control once execution gets
    back to the outermost loop - it doesn't preempt an already-running
    nested one, so the quit request just sat there forever with
    nothing to act on it. The tray's Quit handler now forces every
    currently-open dialog to respond before quitting, which unwinds
    the nested loop and lets the real quit proceed - confirmed live
    end to end (Preferences open, Quit closes it and the whole app).
    Affects both X11 and Wayland alike, since the handler is shared
    code, not platform-specific.

